<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andhra Mahabharatam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th–14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Andhra Mahabharatam is the Telugu version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, written by three poets known as the Kavitrayam. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nannaya">Nannaya</a> began it in the 11th century at the court of the Eastern Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-narendra">Rajaraja Narendra</a>, Tikkana continued it in the 13th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/errana">Errana</a> completed it in the 14th century under the Reddi kings. It is regarded as the founding work of Telugu literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andhra-mahabharatam.jpg" alt="Andhra Mahabharatam" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: bazaar art · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bazaar_art_print_of_Bhavadgita,_c._mid-1900%27s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
